{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>Until I come to my lord at Seir.</b> It was not Jacob's intention to go back to Seir, but his desire was to distance himself from him; therefore he made the way long for him. And this is what the Rabbis, of blessed memory, said: should one be asked, 'Where are you going?' he should make the way long, in the manner that Jacob distanced himself from Esau." "<b>And in the Midrash:</b> 'Until I come to my lord at Seir' — we do not find that Jacob ever went to Seir, but in the days of the Messiah he will fulfill his word, as it is said (Obadiah 1:21): ״ועלו מושיעים בהר ציון לשפוט את הר עשו״. And therefore you will find in the final letters of the words a hint of <b>Elijah</b>, as it is written (Malachi 3:23): הנה אנכי שולח לכם את אליה הנביא."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 33, :notes {1 "The rabbinic saying in comment 0 is paraphrased from Bavli Ta'anit; the exact phrase 'ירחיב לו הדרך' matches the idiom there. The acrostic in comment 1 refers to the last letters of words in the phrase 'עד אשר אבא אל אדני שעירה' spelling אליה in reverse or order — the calculation is reproduced exactly as R. Bahya gives it."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:17:26.848451643Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}